## Debrief

Quality Counts hired me to redesign and brand an experimental tool and bring it to production status. To accomplish this task, I created a design system called Cardinal. By zooming into individual interface elements and zooming out, as I discuss in my [process page](https://www.notion.so/Process-872b0bcd115e44ee8beca49eff0716ad), I was able to refine both the product's experience and how the interface elements come together. The resulting tool helps citizens and city planners grow their communities.

The team successfully delivered an updated version of DataPoint (2.0), which includes advanced planning features to understand if sections of a road were at, over, or under capacity based on historical traffic data. With the creation of Cardinal, the team was able to experiment with other ideas quickly to find product market fit, such as using computer vision systems to count traffic instead of the tubes traditionally found across roads. It was a pleasure to guide the team in creating a vision for what DataPoint and other software from Quality Counts could look like in the future.

## Problem

Quality Counts (QC) successfully created an experimental self-serve environment for citizens and city planners using their traffic count data via a web-based tool. However, this was their first attempt at creating a production-ready software in terms of performance and user experience. As QC had no experience in software development, they hired me to lead the creative direction and work with internal resources and a contracted development team to create a software division of the company.

The challenge was to create a tool that engineers could use to make informed design decisions, while also being simple enough for citizens to quickly validate assumptions about traffic capacity in their community.

## Goals

1. Create a foundational design system for building DataPoint and other potential software solutions.
2. Redesign the experience and interface of DataPoint.
3. Add a module to DataPoint that can determine the traffic capacity of a selected intersection.

## Process

Within the day to day activates I follow the two different process that I outline on my [process page](https://app.notion.com/p/Process-872b0bcd115e44ee8beca49eff0716ad?pvs=21). To give a bit more context of what these processes looked like when working on DataPoint you can break it down into the following:

### **Understand**

When I first joined the DataPoint team, I was faced with a steep learning curve as I had no prior experience in city planning, traffic analysis, or navigating public office constraints and relations. To truly grasp the goals of Quality Counts (QC) and their vision for DataPoint, I dedicated my first month to studying the existing version of the product and engaging in extensive conversations with QC team members. Additionally, I reached out to multiple cities that had previously seen a demo of DataPoint to identify their desired features and functionality that would improve their workflow processes.

Based on my research, I concluded that there was a dual need that had to be addressed. While stakeholders needed to be informed of their cities' traffic conditions, they also craved the capability to analyze and comprehend the root causes of specific traffic issues at intersections or midblocks. I synthesized this data from all stakeholders and collaborated with the core development team of DataPoint and QC members to define the scope of a new and improved version of DataPoint.

Through diligent research and collaboration with key stakeholders, we were able to identify a clear direction for the development of DataPoint, which ultimately led to a more effective and user-friendly version of the product.

### **Explore**

To optimize DataPoint 2.0, I delved into two key areas: refining its visual identity and exploring the diverse mental models of interaction that our customers have. This entailed examining whether our users prefer navigating data via a map, searching for specific stations, or viewing station data before returning to the bigger picture. As I ventured further into these domains, I realized that there were other areas that demanded a more thorough understanding before crafting interfaces. To address this, I collaborated with the team to identify the most critical experiences with the data we had acquired so far, while also compiling an opportunity backlog of items we wanted to process through the entire design process.

### **Materialize**

When we started to move into areas of the project that we could focus on materializing the design system and interface elements we defined some core principles for what we wanted our interface and interactions to follow.

1. Native to the web → During the understanding section of this project, I conducted a competitive analysis of the tools used by city planners and engineers. I also educated myself on the experiences of everyday citizens would have when viewing map information on platforms such as Google and Apple Maps. As we moved away from the consumer demographic, we noticed that the city planners and engineers were using almost exclusively native Windows apps that followed the software design paradigms of Windows XP. A keyword that we kept hearing from stakeholders was "modern". They wanted this information to be available on any computer with an internet connection. With this in mind, we aimed to create an interface and experience that felt like it belonged on the web, rather than a desktop app that was poorly ported to run in the browser.
2. Minimal → The star of the show was the traffic data. We wanted the interface to melt away, but when displaying data do so in an opinionated way that would make sense for any knowledge level of user.
3. Progressive Disclosure → We learned that there were two common usage patterns. 1. Quickly check and retrieve information from your stations. 2. Take a deep dive into the traffic data. These means that each experience need to have an escape hatch in mind to allow our city planners/engineers download the data and do some manual crunching within Excel.

## Design

To keep these article as concise as possible I will break apart this section into the “Original” version of DataPoint and a 2.0 section that goes over some of the highlights of the changes we made. If you are curious and want to explore I will provide a link to a collection of screenshots of the original DataPoint and a link to the Figma document. If you would like to ask a specific question about this project please reach out to me using the contact page and I will reply as soon as possible.

### **Original**

Unfortunately, the designer who created the base design for DataPoint left before I joined the team. From what I could gather, the primary interaction model is designed for customers to navigate around the map and change their zoom level to select a specific intersection or midblock. Stations that are close together form a cluster and are displayed with a number. When the customer clicks on the cluster, the map zooms in to show each specific station.

When a customer clicks on a specific station, a panel appears with overview information. The information displayed depends on whether the station is an intersection or midblock. This section will cover intersections. The overview shows the most recent types of data and a dropdown that contains each count file. Counts are broken down by time of day, such as AM, Midday, or PM. After selecting a time period, the count data is displayed based on turning movements (breakdown of traffic through each direction of an intersection), truck percentages, pedestrian volume, and bike movements. Below this diagram, there is additional metadata that comes with the count file. If the customer wants to dive into the details, they can press the “View All Details” button, which opens a breakdown of the information in a new tab.



