### **DataPoint 2.0 (Re-design)**

After testing various layouts, our team decided to divide the app into three main sections. Firstly, the top navigation bar would serve as a tool for users to search and filter through items, allowing them to easily locate specific stations. Secondly, the map view would enable both public citizens and authenticated city workers to visually access data related to intersections, midblocks, traffic signals, bus stops, and crash data, using specific iconography. Lastly, the inspector bar would be contextual, changing based on the user's interactions with the first two areas. Without selecting a station, the inspector bar displays a complete report of all visible stations on the selected map. Once a customer chooses a specific station, the inspector area adjusts to show the data of the selected station.

Overall, the app is designed to optimize the initial interaction, where users can quickly browse through data and make informed decisions. For those wanting a more in-depth analysis, we use progressive disclosure by opening a modal on top of the current view.


### Intersections

The modal for the intersection is divided into three tabs, each containing valuable information. The first tab, Station Details, presents an overview of the intersection's basic information, alongside all the necessary files and count data pertinent to the station. The second tab, Traffic Counts, is designed to display data commonly used by city planners and engineers. The tab includes filter controls to select specific count dates and times, as well as diagrams that demonstrate turning movements, the number of trucks and pedestrians, and bike movements for each gate. Additionally, we provide a specific readout of all the count data based on the count time, highlighting peak 15 and peak hour to understand and plan for congestion in the road network.

The third tab, Capacity Planner and Simulator, includes a formula based on the "Highway Capacity Manual" from the engineers at Kittelson, to help understand when an intersection is below, near, or over capacity. This tab includes a basic mode for new engineers and an advanced mode that provides extensive control over every part of the formula. Moreover, the top bar of the modal features an affordance to export the data and an icon to open the station information in a new tab, facilitating side-by-side comparisons with other stations.



### Midblocks

Compared to intersections, midblocks only exhibit traffic flow in two directions, either North and South or East and West. Additionally, midblocks have two tabs. The first tab shares similarities with the intersection, providing information on basic station details and file management. The second tab, on the other hand, offers a more detailed breakdown of the traffic counts, presenting useful charts and filters that enable customers to have a better understanding of the types of vehicles that are passing through the station.

