### Everything else

I poured a lot of effort and attention into every aspect of the DataPoint project. One of my primary focuses was streamlining the upload and management processes, ensuring they were user-friendly and intuitive. In addition, I collaborated with the development team to incorporate Intercom within the app, which helped make the customer support experience seamless, particularly for new users who were onboarding.

## Next steps

The launch of DataPoint 2.0 was a success, although there were some challenges along the way. When I last spoke with Quality Counts, they had a queue of about 30 customers waiting to be onboarded. One area that requires additional work is data management and importing. To simplify the process, Quality Counts offers a data import service, which involves QC employees taking historical data and importing it directly into the DataPoint instance. However, this approach has its challenges, since older data can be very messy. Improving the efficiency of data importing and problem-solving with internal tools could eliminate this issue and allow us to focus our attention on ensuring customer data is clean.

Another area that requires attention is the technical aspect of the platform. Currently, each customer has their own DataPoint instance, which means there is a unique URL for each city. I suggested the idea of creating a unified instance for all customers, which would enable different cities to share traffic data and collaborate effectively. This idea was postponed for the initial release of DataPoint 2.0 but is part of our roadmap for the 3.0 release.

As more customers use DataPoint, we need to re-evaluate whether our current progressive disclosure modal still makes sense. We may need to store a few stations in a minimized state in a bottom interaction bar, or add a windowing feature that enables customers to view multiple stations' data within the same browser window, rather than opening each station in a new tab.

## Takeaways

Designing a tool that caters to both novice and pro users can be a daunting task. However, I discovered that implementing a progressive disclosure approach proved to be effective for this project. It's important to bear in mind that just because the target audience is pro users, it doesn't imply that the tool should be overly intricate or packed with information. Instead, providing on and off ramps within the user's current view or mental modal enables them to access the relevant information they need without getting overwhelmed.

